  "step_3": "The decision reason emphasizes preferring the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., the one with greater probability on unfavorable outcomes), which in this case is Lottery B. However, the individual’s reasoning does not reflect any consideration of the distribution's shape or skewness. Instead, the individual focuses on the higher prize amount and dismisses the small probability difference. While the individual might end up choosing Lottery B (higher prize, lower probability), their justification is based on wanting to 'win more', not on a preference for more right-skewed distributions. There is no alignment between the information used in the decision reason (distribution skewness) and the information considered by the individual (prize magnitude and probability difference as negligible). The justification (wanting a bigger win) contradicts the logic of preferring more right-skewed lotteries, which typically involves accepting worse odds for a payoff — but not because one likes the skew, but because one likes the big prize. Thus, the reasoning process is outcome-motivated, not distribution-motivated. The decision reason is not reflected in the verbal report.",
